######## BPTT (Backprop Through Time)
[tagline] "Unroll, walk back, and find out what the window really bounds."
[topicId] "bptt"
[whatIsIt[0]] "Backpropagation through time is ordinary backpropagation applied to a network that was written as a loop. Unroll a recurrent layer over T timesteps and what you have is a T-layer feedforward network in which every layer shares one weight matrix. The forward pass is the loop you wrote; the backward pass walks from the loss at the end of the sequence to the first step, multiplying by that same matrix on the way, and the gradient for the shared weights is the *sum* of what every step contributed. Nothing about it is special except the sharing, and the sharing is where all the trouble lives."
[whatIsIt[1]] "The trouble is measurable rather than folkloric. In the lab's task — one informative token, nine steps of noise, a decision at the end — the gradient arriving at the last step has norm 0.480 at initialization and the one arriving at the first step has norm 0.0085, 56× smaller after nine multiplications. The first step's contribution to the recurrent matrix is not merely small, it is exactly zero, because h₀ is the zero vector and that step's outer product has a zero factor in it. The cue reaches the input matrix and the recurrent matrix never sees it at all."
[whatIsIt[2]] "So the standard remedy is truncation: keep the forward pass whole, stop the gradient after k steps, and store k activations instead of T. That is how every recurrent model on long sequences is actually trained, and it is usually described as a cheap approximation. Measured on this task it is something stranger. A truncated gradient is 99% cosine-aligned with the full one at k = 3, and can even be *larger* than it. It is also, at that width, useless: across three initializations the k = 3 model learns the rule once and fails twice. The alignment and the learning have almost nothing to do with each other."
[steps[0]] {"number": 1, "title": "Unroll", "body": "T timesteps become T layers sharing one weight matrix."}
[steps[1]] {"number": 2, "title": "Walk Back", "body": "One multiplication by Wᵀ per step, from the loss to the start."}
[steps[2]] {"number": 3, "title": "Sum the Contributions", "body": "∂L/∂W is a sum over steps — not one term, T of them."}
[steps[3]] {"number": 4, "title": "Watch It Decay", "body": "0.480 at the output, 0.0085 nine steps earlier: 56×."}
[steps[4]] {"number": 5, "title": "Truncate", "body": "Cut the walk at k steps: k activations stored instead of T."}
[steps[5]] {"number": 6, "title": "Check What Was Cut", "body": "Gradient similarity says almost nothing about what gets learned."}
